# Gitleaks false-positive whitelist.
#
# Each line is a single fingerprint:
#     <commit-sha>:<file>:<rule-id>:<line>
# emitted by `gitleaks` itself on a finding. Add a fingerprint here
# when (and only when) the finding is a verified false positive — a
# placeholder, a documented redaction example, etc. — that we've
# decided not to rewrite history for.
#
# Keep one comment block per entry explaining WHY it's a false
# positive, so future maintainers don't have to re-investigate.

# docs/debugging.md "Reproducing a failing request" curl example —
# the original wording used `YOUR_REAL_TOKEN_HERE` as a placeholder,
# which the `curl-auth-header` rule flagged because its entropy
# (3.34) crossed the rule's threshold. The placeholder was a clear
# documentation aid, not a real token. The wording was rewritten in
# commit 4671f59 to use `$TOKEN` (shell-variable expansion) which
# gitleaks accepts and which is also more accurate guidance — but the
# original commit `b312358` still lives in the branch / PR history,
# so we whitelist that specific finding here.
